nglish auxiliary verbs","word":"do"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"does"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"did"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"have"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"has"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"had"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"may"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"might"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"must"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"shall"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"should"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"will"},{"sense":"examples of English auxiliary verbs","word":"would"},{"word":"Other types of verbs: Thesaurus:verb"}],"synonyms":[]},"auxiliary":{"id":"3ffb49b7-2911-4616-bd48-906cf0815ebb","word":"auxiliary","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"person or group that acts in an auxiliary manner","word":"auxiliar"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Auxiliaries and ancillaries are comprehensive, and include a Westinghouse motor-driven recriprocating compressor used for locomotive braking and general service air, two rotary exhauster sets for train brakes when hauling passenger or fitted freight trains, and an oil-fired train heating boiler.","ref":"1950 November, “Mixed-Traffic Diesel-Electric Locomotives for Ireland”, in Railway Magazine, page 781:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[0,11]],"id":"ff8e2588-c805-45f4-b86f-7dd6d167156e"},{"text":"A drive to exploit the Highlands as a winter sports area, with all the necessary auxiliaries, such as chair lifts in the Cairngorms, shows increasing promise, […].","ref":"1962 September, G. Freeman Allen, “The New Look in Scotland's Northern Division—1”, in Modern Railways, page 163:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[81,92]],"id":"ac031bcc-055a-4efd-8347-3a20fce4539f"},{"text":"Summer “felt betrayed” that a close friend had used AI as “an auxiliary” to talk to her.","ref":"2025 June 30, Adrienne Matei, “‘Hey man, I’m so sorry for your loss’: should you use AI to text?”, in The Guardian, →ISSN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[62,71]],"id":"ca95e20f-a869-419a-9798-934f7a5f6aed"}],"links":[["person","person"],["group","group"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A person or group that acts in an auxiliary manner."],"id":"05abe8d0-425d-40d4-811a-5c187d3a67f6"},{"topics":["nautical","transport"],"categories":["en:Nautical"],"links":[["nautical","nautical"],["vessel","vessel"]],"raw_glosses":["(nautical) A sailing vessel equipped with an engine."],"glosses":["A sailing vessel equipped with an engine."],"id":"016b7ae7-0f2a-4fe8-b934-ae6e1b57a1c9"},{"examples":[{"text":"The three traditionally recognized Non-modal Auxiliaries are the per-\nfective Auxiliary have, the progressive Auxiliary be, and the passive Auxiliary\nbe. Perfective have is so-called because it marks the completion (hence, perfec-\ntion) of an action; it is followed by a VP headed by a perfective -n participle, as\nin:\n(121) The referee has [_(VP) shown him the red card]","ref":"1988, Andrew Radford, Transformational grammar: a first course, Cambridge, UK: Cambridge University Press, page 151:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[45,56],[78,87],[110,119],[140,149]],"id":"b1304ab4-9d19-48ad-a4d9-f8273bba0007"}],"topics":["grammar","human-sciences","linguistics","sciences"],"categories":["English ellipses","English terms with quotations","en:Grammar"],"links":[["grammar","grammar"],["auxiliary verb","auxiliary verb#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(grammar) Ellipsis of auxiliary verb."],"glosses":["Ellipsis of auxiliary verb."],"tags":["abbreviation","alt-of","ellipsis"],"alt_of":[{"word":"auxiliary verb"}],"id":"9a704d37-d156-4e5c-ac91-f342e74c994c"},{"links":[["marching band","marching band"],["color guard","color guard"]],"glosses":["A marching band color guard."],"id":"fef67437-b3f9-459d-bcd3-c71b6cbe9426"}],"related":[{"word":"auction"},{"word":"auctioneer"},{"word":"augment"},{"word":"augmentation"},{"word":"axillary"}],"synonyms":[]},"auxin":{"id":"0d61e638-50fb-4112-a108-89e9467c4362","word":"auxin","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"class of plant growth hormones","tags":["feminine"],"word":"auxina"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"In Arabidopsis roots, cold stress may reduce shootward auxin transport and alter the intracellular auxin gradient.","ref":"2015 July 2, Yang Y-W, Chen H-C, Jen W-F, Liu L-Y, Chang M-C, “Comparative Transcriptome Analysis of Shoots and Roots of TNG67 and TCN1 Rice Seedlings under Cold Stress and Following Subsequent Recovery: Insights into Metabolic Pathways, Phytohormones, and Transcription Factors”, in PLOS ONE, volume 10, number 7, →DOI:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[55,60],[99,104]],"id":"6faca80c-8a0a-44fa-a223-a12bd2291aa6"},{"text":"SE onset depends on a complex network of interactions among plant growth regulators, mainly auxins and cytokinins, during the proembryogenic early stages, and ethylene and gibberellic and abscisic acids later in the development of somatic embryos.","ref":"2015 August 18, Clelia De-la-Peña et al., “The role of chromatin modifications in somatic embryogenesis in plants”, in Frontiers in Plant Science, volume 6, →DOI:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[92,98]],"id":"66a71b4e-deb1-4151-9f2f-a8caee87d2d1"}],"topics":["biochemistry","biology","botany","chemistry","microbiology","natural-sciences","physical-sciences"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from Ancient Greek","English terms derived from Proto-Indo-European","English terms derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *h₂ewg-","English terms suffixed with -in","English terms with quota